1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,900
Good morning, everyone.

2
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:05,877
In the last few lectures I've been dealing with business finance,

3
00:00:05,877 --> 00:00:09,450
but now I'm going to move on to business systems.

4
00:00:09,450 --> 00:00:14,307
And in today's lecture I'm going to talk about what can go wrong when

5
00:00:14,307 --> 00:00:17,900
businesses try to copy their own best practices.

6
00:00:17,900 --> 00:00:25,110
Once a business has successfully introduced a new process - managing a branch bank, say,

7
00:00:25,110 --> 00:00:27,480
or selling a new product -

8
00:00:27,480 --> 00:00:32,203
the parent organisation naturally wants to repeat that success,

9
00:00:32,203 --> 00:00:35,560
and capture it if possible on a bigger scale.

10
00:00:35,560 --> 00:00:43,360
The goal, then, is to utilise existing knowledge and not to generate new knowledge.

11
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:48,502
It's a less glamorous activity than pure innovation,

12
00:00:48,502 --> 00:00:52,520
but it actually happens more often, as a matter of fact.

13
00:00:52,520 --> 00:00:59,926
However, surprisingly, getting things right the Second time is not necessarily any simpler than

14
00:00:59,926 --> 00:01:01,110
it was the first time.

15
00:01:01,110 --> 00:01:07,948
Now, there's been a lot of research into how companies can repeat their previous

16
00:01:07,948 --> 00:01:08,610
successes,

17
00:01:08,610 --> 00:01:12,240
and it certainly hasn't been confined to the United States.

18
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:18,415
It seems that most large industries are trying to repeat their own successes,

19
00:01:18,415 --> 00:01:24,370
and manage the knowledge they've acquired - but even so it has been shown

20
00:01:24,370 --> 00:01:28,400
that the overwhelming majority of attempts fail.

21
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:34,216
A host of studies confirm this, covering a wide range of business settings:

22
00:01:34,216 --> 00:01:42,198
branch banks, retail stores, real estate agencies, factories, call centres.., to name

23
00:01:42,198 --> 00:01:42,800
but a few.

24
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:49,680
So why do so few managers get things right the second or third time?

25
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:55,677
Let's consider one reason for failure - placing too much trust in the people

26
00:01:55,677 --> 00:02:00,490
who are running the successful operation, the 'experts' shall we say.

27
00:02:00,490 --> 00:02:06,848
Managers who want to apply existing knowledge typically start off by going to an

28
00:02:06,848 --> 00:02:11,983
expert - such as the person who designed and is running a successful department store -

29
00:02:11,983 --> 00:02:14,290
and picking their brains.

30
00:02:14,290 --> 00:02:19,825
Now, this approach can be used if you want to gain a rough understanding

31
00:02:19,825 --> 00:02:21,149
of a particular system,

32
00:02:21,149 --> 00:02:25,260
or understand smaller, isolated problems.

33
00:02:25,260 --> 00:02:30,913
The trouble is, even the expert doesn't fully grasp the whole thing

34
00:02:30,913 --> 00:02:37,247
because when it comes to complex systems, the individual components of the process are

35
00:02:37,247 --> 00:02:38,670
interwoven with one another.

36
00:02:38,670 --> 00:02:43,680
The expert never has complete access to the necessary information.

37
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:51,674
And the situation's complicated even further by the fact that experts are usually not

38
00:02:51,674 --> 00:02:53,200
aware of their own ignorance.

39
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:56,871
The ignorance can take various forms.

40
00:02:56,871 --> 00:03:02,910
For instance, a lot of details of the system are invisible to managers.

41
00:03:02,910 --> 00:03:07,250
Some may be difficult to describe - learned on the job

42
00:03:07,250 --> 00:03:09,854
and well known by workers perhaps,

43
00:03:09,854 --> 00:03:13,760
but impossible to describe in a way that's helpful.

44
00:03:13,760 --> 00:03:19,649
And there are some things that people know or do that they're not even

45
00:03:19,649 --> 00:03:20,490
aware of.

46
00:03:20,490 --> 00:03:29,235
Now, let's consider two types of mistake that can occur when a manager actually

47
00:03:29,235 --> 00:03:36,730
starts to set up a duplicate system to replicate a successful process.

48
00:03:36,730 --> 00:03:43,360
Firstly, perhaps he forgets that he was just trying to copy another process,

49
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:46,930
and starts trying to improve on it.

50
00:03:46,930 --> 00:03:53,490
Another mistake is trying to use the best parts of various different systems, in

51
00:03:53,490 --> 00:03:56,770
the hope of creating the perfect combination.

52
00:03:56,770 --> 00:04:05,500
Unfortunately, attempts like these usually turn out to be misguided and lead to problems.

53
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:05,550
Why?

54
00:04:05,550 --> 00:04:08,556
Well, for various reasons.

55
00:04:08,556 --> 00:04:13,444
Perhaps there weren't really any advantages after all,

56
00:04:13,444 --> 00:04:16,500
because the information wasn't accurate.

57
00:04:16,500 --> 00:04:21,090
Or perhaps the business settings weren't really comparable.

58
00:04:21,090 --> 00:04:25,378
More typically, the advantages are real enough,

59
00:04:25,378 --> 00:04:30,890
but there are also disadvantages that have been overlooked.

60
00:04:30,890 --> 00:04:37,280
For example, the modifications might compromise safety in some way.

61
00:04:37,280 --> 00:04:39,375
So, what's the solution?

62
00:04:39,375 --> 00:04:46,705
Well, I don't intend to suggest that it's easy to get things right the

63
00:04:46,705 --> 00:04:48,800
second time.., it's not.

64
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:54,686
But the underlying problem has more to do with attitudes than the actual difficulty

65
00:04:54,686 --> 00:04:55,947
of the task,

66
00:04:55,947 --> 00:04:59,310
and there are ways of getting it right.

67
00:04:59,310 --> 00:05:09,100
These involve adjusting attitudes, first of all.., being more realistic and cautious really.

68
00:05:09,100 --> 00:05:18,000
Secondly, they involve exerting strict controls on the organisational and operational systems.

69
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:24,060
And this in turn means copying the original as closely as possible.

70
00:05:24,060 --> 00:05:29,275
Not merely duplicating the physical characteristics of the factory,

71
00:05:29,275 --> 00:05:35,070
but also duplicating the skills that the original employees had.

72
00:05:35,070 --> 00:05:44,110
Reliance on a template like this offers the huge advantage of built-in consistency.
